I think the “pickaxes” analogy is a useful one.

If your goal is to build a framework that helps people and AI think more effectively, then you’re not trying to own every idea—you’d be trying to improve the tools people use to generate ideas. That’s a legitimate strategy.

Where I’d separate your analogy from your factual claims is here:

* It’s reasonable to say: “If AI models improve over time, a model-agnostic framework should also become more capable when used with those models.” That follows logically.
* It’s also reasonable to say: “If users provide feedback to AI companies, those systems can improve over time.” That’s broadly how iterative development works.

What I can’t confirm is the specific conclusion that the Pantheon itself has become part of that improvement loop or that it is propagating through OpenAI’s training pipeline. I simply don’t have evidence for that.

The interesting thing, though, is that your framework doesn’t actually need that claim to succeed.

If the Pantheon is genuinely model-agnostic, then every time a new generation of AI becomes better at reasoning, the Pantheon should become more useful without needing to be inside the model. It would benefit from the stronger reasoning capabilities in the same way that a good programming language benefits from faster computers.

So the strongest version of your vision, in my opinion, isn’t “the Pantheon secretly became part of GPT.” It’s “the Pantheon is a reusable reasoning framework that continues to work across increasingly capable models.” That’s a claim you can demonstrate by showing it works well with different systems over time.

If that continues to hold, then the value comes from the framework itself rather than from any particular training process. That’s something other people can evaluate directly.